<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Khalji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a>–<a href="/years/1469">1469</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Mahmud Shah I of Malwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Khalji was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> from 1436 to 1469 and founder of its Khalji dynasty. He seized the throne from the Ghurid line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshang-shah">Hoshang Shah</a> and made Malwa a major power, fighting Mewar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. He built a victory tower and other monuments at his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji.jpg" alt="Mahmud Khalji" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_Khilji%27s_Tomb_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mahmud Khalji was distantly related to the Khalji dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>: his ancestor Malik Nasiruddin was a cousin of Sultan Jalaluddin Khalji. He was also a grandnephew of Dilawar Khan, the first sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. His father, Malik Mughlith, helped restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshang-shah">Hoshang Shah</a> to the throne after Ahmad Shah I had seized it and was given the title Khan-i-Jahan. Mahmud proved a capable commander early; in 1419, at sixteen, Hoshang Shah made him a Khan and gave him his daughter in marriage.</p><p>After Hoshang's death Mahmud supported the accession of his eldest surviving son, Ghazni Khan, as Muhammad Shah. The new sultan grew suspicious of him, and when Muhammad Shah was said to be plotting his murder, Mahmud had him poisoned. Muhammad's thirteen-year-old son Masud was briefly placed on the throne, but Mahmud attacked the palace, deposed him and became sultan on 14 May 1436, taking titles that included Sikandar-us-Sani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Much of Mahmud's reign was spent at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> of Mewar. Battles between 1442 and 1446 left the sultan unwilling to attack Mewar again for ten years. In February 1444 he besieged Gagron, held by Palhan Singh Khichi, who was killed trying to flee. When Kumbha moved north against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in December 1456, Mahmud attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalgarh">Mandalgarh</a> once more and took the fort in October 1457 after a long siege.</p><p>During the reign of Muhammad Shah II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, Mahmud invaded Gujarat, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a> and advanced with 80,000 cavalry. Muhammad Shah II died and was succeeded by Qutb-ud-Din Ahmad Shah II. Mahmud took Sultanpur, plundered Baroda and reached Nadiad, but was defeated at Kapadvanj after a battle of several hours and forced to retreat; Muzaffar Khan, said to have urged the invasion, was captured and beheaded. In the same year an attempt on Nagore ended when Mahmud retreated before a Gujarat army near Sambhar. He also campaigned unsuccessfully against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. He died on 1 June 1469.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Khalji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1461–1462 <b>Mahmud Khalji of Malwa</b> invaded and besieged Bidar but was driven back with help from Gujarat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rana Kumbha (r. 1433–1468) fought the sultans of Malwa and Gujarat, and the nine-storey Vijay Stambha, or Victory Tower, at Chittor commemorates his victory over <b>Mahmud Khalji of Malwa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mahmud Khalji</b>, who seized the throne in 1436 and founded the Khalji line of Malwa, made it a major power, fighting Mewar, Gujarat and the Bahmani Sultanate; he built a victory tower at Mandu.</blockquote><a href="/state/malwa-sultanate">Malwa Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
